  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 115 PM CST Wed Nov 18 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery indicated a negatively tilted trough \nlifting over the Northern Plains with a trailing cold front \nprogged to stall across southern Nebraska tonight. A tight \npressure gradient was also noted over the Central Plains with \nstrong south to southwest winds gusting over 40 mph at times. \nThis gradient is progged to relax as we move into Thursday, but \nthis will be short-lived as another mid/upper jet noses into the\nRockies on Friday allowing sfc pressure falls to return to the \nCentral High Plains while a strong pressure gradient develops \ndownstream over the Central Plains once again. Time/height \nX-section of wind/mixing layer height support peak gusts in the \n35-40 knot range, highest in central KS on Fri afternoon.\n\nAs we move into Saturday, a shortwave trough is progged to emerge \nfrom the Rockies late in the period bringing increasing \nprobabilities for showers and perhaps some embedded storms. Lower 50 \ndewpoints are progged to spread northward in the warm sector as the \npv anomaly rotates over the region during the overnight hours. A \nstable post-frontal regime will follow in the wake of the front as \nwe move into Sunday with more seasonable temperatures anticipated Sun-\nMon.\n\nA progressive zonal flow will allow another fast moving shortwave \ntrough to sweep across the Central Plains on Tuesday bringing a \nchance of mixed precipitation to the area as temperatures hover\nnear or just above freezing. Due to the progressive nature of \nthis feature, any precipitation is expected to remain light but \nwe could see a rain/snow mix in parts of central KS early on \nTue. Seasonably cool conditions will linger into Wed with some \nunsettled weather possible returning later in the week.",
